## Lab Steps

### Step 1: Verify Current User and Privileges

```bash
whoami
groups
sudo -v

### Step 2: Create a New Standard User

Create a new user account:
```bash
sudo adduser devuser

Follow the prompts to set a password and optional user details.
Verify user creation:
```bash
id devuser


### Step 3: Verify Default Privilege Level

Switch to the new user:
```bash
su - devuser

Attempt a privileged command:
```bash
sudo apt update

Expected result: Permission denied
Exit back to the original user:
```bash
exit

Step 4: Grant Sudo Access (Least Privilege)

Add the user to the sudo group:
```bash
sudo usermod -aG sudo devuser

Verify group membership:
```bash
groups devuser

Step 5: Test Sudo Access

Switch back to the new user:
```bash
su - devuser

Test sudo functionality:
```bash
sudo apt update
Expected result: command executes successfully after password prompt.

Step 6: Review Sudo Configuration (Read-Only)

Safely inspect sudo configuration:
```bash
sudo visudo
Observe:
%sudo ALL=(ALL:ALL) ALL
Group-based privilege delegation
Exit without making changes.

Step 7: Disable Direct Root Login (Optional but Recommended)

Lock the root account:
```bash
sudo passwd -l root

Verify root login is disabled:
```bash
su -
Expected result: authentication failure.


### Validation & Verification 

Successful completion is confirmed by:
 New user account created successfully
 User initially denied sudo access
 Sudo access granted via group membership
 Privileged commands execute only with sudo
 Root login is disabled or restricted

Optional validation:
 SSH login tested using the new user
 Snapshot created in VirtualBox after configuration


### Security & Operational Considerations

 Root account is not used for daily administration
 Privilege escalation is logged and auditable via sudo
 Group-based access simplifies permission management
 Least-privilege principles reduce blast radius
 User separation supports accountability and auditing

###Lessons Learned

 Linux user management is central to system security
 Sudo provides controlled administrative access without exposing root
 Group-based privilege assignment scales better than per-user rules
 Disabling root login significantly reduces attack surface
 These practices directly align with cloud VM and EC2 instance management
This lab establishes a secure access baseline for future labs involving
network services, automation, and cloud connectivity.

